-- #1110 unit D (`Mono.TCon` carries a module identity).  The GATING SHAPE, not a
-- demonstration of the feature: two modules with NO import relationship each
-- declare a type spelled `Shape`, so after unit D the two head Monos carry
-- DIFFERENT `TyConOrigin`s while every name-equality comparison in the checker
-- still ignores the field.  The assertion is that this changes nothing.
--
-- ⚠️ Read what this pins and what it does not.  A fixture exercising the new
-- carrier would pass by construction — nothing reads the field yet, so no output
-- can depend on it.  What is worth pinning is the opposite: that a program whose
-- heads now disagree about identity behaves exactly as it did when they could not
-- disagree at all.  `shapeone` never imports `shapetwo`, and the FIRST output line
-- is entirely its own — the module that has nothing to do with the collision.
-- (The machinery that changed is in the COMPILER, of course; both modules traverse
-- it identically.  What is "unrelated" here is the two DECLARATIONS to each other.)
--
-- 🚨 IT IS NOT THE PROGRAM THE NEXT UNIT MOVES, and an earlier version of this
-- header claimed it was.  For an origin-reading consumer to change an answer, two
-- differently-originated same-named heads must MEET IN A COMPARISON.  Here they
-- never do: each `Shape` is built and consumed entirely inside its own module,
-- `main` only ever handles the `Int`/`String` they hand back, and neither module
-- declares an `impl`, so no `Shape`-headed `Mono` is ever a dispatch goal.  This
-- will print `3`/`e` through the next unit and name nothing.
--
-- The program with THAT property already exists and already self-drains:
-- `test/must_fail_fixtures/1208-xmod-type-name-collision/` — two modules' `T`s
-- unify because the head carries no identity, `medaka check` exits 0 with zero
-- diagnostics, and the must-fail gate goes RED the day a consumer makes it reject.
-- Watch that one for the acceptance change; watch this one for the byte-identical
-- behaviour of code standing next to it.
--
-- Expected output, derived by hand before capture:
--   mkOne 3   = Corner 3 6   → oneSpan = 6 - 3   = 3
--   mkTwo "e" = Edge "e"     → twoLabel = "e", and `println` on a String prints
--                              it unquoted
import shapeone.{mkOne, oneSpan}
import shapetwo.{mkTwo, twoLabel}

main =
  println (oneSpan (mkOne 3))
  println (twoLabel (mkTwo "e"))
